<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Campaign</p><h1 class="enc-title">Italian campaign of 1796–1797</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1796">1796</a>–<a href="/years/1797">1797</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Italian campaign of 1796–1797 was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon Bonaparte</a>'s first great command, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a>. In April 1796 he knocked the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> out of the war, then defeated the Austrians at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a>, Arcole and Rivoli and took Mantua. The campaign ended with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> in 1797, which created French client republics in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/italian-campaign-of-1796-1797.jpg" alt="Italian campaign of 1796–1797" width="320" height="241" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Angelus · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paintings_of_Napoleon_I_on_the_battlefield.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>By the spring of 1794 the Army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> had been at war for two years with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a> of Victor Amadeus III. Bonaparte, made a brigadier general after the victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toulon">Toulon</a> in 1793, served it as inspector of artillery, and in 1795 he urged the reconquest of Vado Ligure and Ceva. On 27 March 1796 he took command, with Sérurier, Augereau and Masséna as his senior generals of division. The Austrians under Johann Peter Beaulieu and their Sardinian allies had about 54,500 men, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> treated Italy as a secondary front to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The campaign opened with the Montenotte campaign on 10 April 1796, and after the victory at Mondovì Sardinia was forced out of the war. Bonaparte chased the Austrians across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lombardy">Lombardy</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lodi-dynasty">Lodi</a> on 10 May, put down revolts in Pavia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, and in June began the long siege of Mantua, defeating four Austrian relief efforts between August 1796 and January 1797. Meanwhile his forces invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modena">Modena</a> and Reggio, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> and the Papal States, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-pius-vi">Pope Pius VI</a> bought the armistice of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bologna">Bologna</a> with territory, money and works of art.</p><p>After the destruction of the last relief army at Rivoli in January 1797, Mantua surrendered on 2 February, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tolentino">Treaty of Tolentino</a> with the Papal States followed on 19 February. Bonaparte then crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, reaching <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/klagenfurt">Klagenfurt</a> by the end of March, and drove on into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> until the Peace of Leoben of 18 April 1797. Its terms gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> to Austria, and Bonaparte dissolved the Republic of Venice on 12 May.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Directory was shocked by the terms of Leoben but accepted them. When elections gave the conservatives a supermajority, Bonaparte sent troops under Augereau to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and supplied evidence against Jean-Charles Pichegru, helping the Directory purge the legislature in the Coup of 18 Fructidor on 4 September 1797. Ignoring the Directory's order not to give up <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a>, he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-campo-formio">Treaty of Campo Formio</a> on 17 October 1797, ending the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-first-coalition">War of the First Coalition</a>.</p><p>The campaign was his first in command of a whole army and made him well known throughout <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Directory's representative Henri Jacques Guillaume Clarke wrote that there was nobody there who did not look upon him as a man of genius.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Italian campaign of 1796–1797” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
